              "text": "How long sliced American cheese lasts after buying depends heavily on whether you purchased **individually wrapped processed slices** (like Kraft Singles) or **freshly sliced cheese from the deli counter**. \n\n---\n\n### 1. Individually Wrapped Slices (e.g., Kraft Singles)\nBecause these slices are highly processed, pasteurized, and individually sealed in plastic, they have an exceptionally long shelf life.\n* **Unopened package:** Can last **1 to 2 months** in the refrigerator *beyond* the printed \"best-by\" or \"sell-by\" date.\n* **Opened package:** Once the main outer package is opened, the individually sealed slices will still last **1 to 2 months** in the fridge. \n\n### 2. Freshly Sliced Deli American Cheese\nAmerican cheese sliced fresh at the deli counter is exposed to the air during slicing and is typically wrapped only in butcher paper or a plastic bag. Because of this, it spoils much faster:\n* **Unopened/Opened:** It will remain safe and of good quality in the refrigerator for **2 to 3 weeks**. \n* **For Best Quality:** Many deli brands (such as Land O\u2019 Lakes) recommend consuming freshly sliced deli cheese within **5 to 7 days** of purchase for the best flavor and texture.\n\n---\n\n### Can You Freeze Sliced American Cheese?\n**Yes, but with a catch.** You can freeze sliced American cheese to extend its shelf life for **up to 6 to 8 months**. \n* **The drawback:** Freezing changes the molecular structure of the cheese, often making it dry, crumbly, or prone to breaking apart when thawed. \n* **Best use:** Thawed American cheese is not great for cold sandwiches, but it is still excellent for melting into hot dishes like macaroni and cheese, grilled cheese, sauces, or burgers. \n* **How to thaw:** Always thaw frozen cheese slowly in the refrigerator for 24 hours before using.\n\n---\n\n### Signs That Sliced American Cheese Has Gone Bad\nWhile processed cheese is highly resistant to spoiling, it can still go bad. Discard the cheese immediately if you notice any of the following:\n* **Mold:** If you see any green, blue, or white mold spots, throw the *entire* package away. (Unlike hard blocks of cheese where you can cut mold off, mold spores spread easily through soft, pre-sliced cheese).\n* **Smell:** A sour, yeast-like, or ammonia-like odor is a clear sign of spoilage.\n* **Texture:** Slices that feel slimy, unusually watery, or completely hardened and shriveled should be discarded.\n* **Taste:** If it tastes sour or bitter, spit it out and throw the package away. \n\n### Best Storage Tips\n* Keep the cheese in the main body or drawer of the refrigerator (ideally between 35\u00b0F and 40\u00b0F). Avoid storing it in the refrigerator door, as temperature fluctuations there accelerate spoilage.\n* For deli-sliced cheese, keep it tightly sealed in a zip-top bag or wrapped in aluminum foil to prevent it from drying out.",
